#!/bin/sh
#
# @name MuJoCo Coaxial X8 + Arm (SITL, MAVLink HIL)
#
# @type Octorotor Coax
#
# GENERATED by scripts/urdf_to_mjcf.py --emit-airframe from
# px4/mujoco_x8.airframe.template. Do not hand-edit: the allocation block below
# is derived from the conversion sidecar, and an edit here would make the
# airframe and the MJCF disagree while both stay flyable.
#
# Airframe for mujoco_px4_sitl. Modelled on 22001_mujoco_quad, whose derivations
# it inherits; the differences are the rotor count, the coaxial layout, the
# measured motor coefficients, and attitude gains raised where its inertia needs.
#
# With PX4_SIMULATOR unset, SYS_AUTOSTART non-zero and not 10017, and SIM_GZ_EN
# at its default of 0, every branch of px4-rc.simulator's if-chain fails and the
# else is taken, which sources px4-rc.mavlinksim. So do NOT set SIM_GZ_EN here.
#
# IMU_INTEG_RATE needs no entry: px4-rc.simulator already pins it to 250 for
# every posix simulator. 250 Hz is the rate the simulator must match.
#
# The CA_ROTOR* geometry below must match the MJCF exactly. Positions here are
# FRD (y right); the model states the same points in FLU (y left), so every PY
# here is the negation of the model's py. KM is positive for CCW.
#
# PZ is omitted throughout, and that is not an oversight: thrust is along body
# +z, so r x [0,0,T] uses only x and y. The spacing between the decks
# contributes nothing to roll or pitch, in our model and in PX4's allocator alike
# (MODELING_CONVENTIONS.md section 4). The decks differ in KM sign, not geometry.
#
# CT is the isolated rotor's thrust at full command on every rotor, including
# the lower deck the sidecar discounts with ct_factor for coaxial interference.
# PX4's pseudo-inverse splits collective thrust in proportion to CT and then
# normalizes by the mean (ControlAllocationPseudoInverse::
# updateControlAllocationMatrixScale), so a discounted lower-deck CT would drive
# the upper deck harder and move the hover point below the MPC_THR_HOVER
# derived here: 0.2427 against 0.2455 on the first X8 at 0.80. Equal CT keeps
# the allocation even, which is what the hover and gain derivations assume, and
# it is how a real vehicle is configured: from the datasheet, with the
# interference a plant property the controller has to absorb.

. ${R}etc/init.d/rc.mc_defaults

# 0 is the generic multirotor geometry: the CA_ROTOR* entries below define the
# layout, rather than selecting a canned one that would not match the CAD.
param set-default CA_AIRFRAME 0

{{ROTOR_BLOCK}}
{{PWM_BLOCK}}
# Declarative only: none of the sensor_*_sim modules reads its own SENS_EN_*SIM,
# and our .post starts all three unconditionally. Set so that `param show
# SENS_EN_*` reflects reality and the airframe matches how sihsim/gzsim express
# the same intent.
param set-default SENS_EN_BAROSIM 1
param set-default SENS_EN_MAGSIM 1
param set-default SENS_EN_GPSSIM 1

# Rotor speed is an explicit state in the model (MODELING_CONVENTIONS.md 2.5):
# omega = omega_idle + (omega_max - omega_idle) * u, T = c_t * omega^2, with a
# non-zero idle speed because an armed motor does not stop at zero command.
#
# These three parameters are one setting, not three. Changing any one alone
# leaves the vehicle flyable but mistuned, which is why they are adjacent.
#
# THR_MDL_FAC 1 is required for consistency with PX4's own rotor model, not
# merely to linearize ours: the allocator's actuator variable is u^2, and
# FunctionMotors' sqrt at fac = 1 is what converts it back to u. At fac = 0 every
# torque comes out exactly 2.00x what the allocator asked for -- flyable, inside
# PX4's default gain margin, which is why it needed asserting rather than
# trusting. 22001_mujoco_quad carries the full derivation and the PX4 source
# lines; MODELING_CONVENTIONS.md 2.6 owns the argument. Unchanged here: it is a
# property of the definitions, not of the platform.
#
# MPC_THR_HOVER is in the allocator's domain, so it is u^2, not u. It depends on
# omega_idle / omega_max and thrust-to-weight and nothing else, and both are
# measured here rather than constructed -- which is why this value differs from
# the quad's 0.2025 despite the same rotor model.
#
# MPC_THR_MIN must move with it, and forgetting this is the trap: the default
# 0.12 is a floor on the *setpoint*, so at fac = 1 it clamps the command at
# sqrt(0.12) = 0.346. 0.12^2 = 0.0144 restores the same command floor. It is the
# quad's value because it encodes the floor, not the hover point.
param set-default THR_MDL_FAC 1.0
param set-default MPC_THR_HOVER {{MPC_THR_HOVER}}
param set-default MPC_THR_MIN 0.0144

# Attitude loops. PX4's rate controllers output a normalized torque, and the
# allocator scales each axis so that +/-1 is this vehicle's own authority on it
# (ControlAllocationPseudoInverse::updateControlAllocationMatrixScale). The
# physical loop gain is therefore PX4's gain times the plant gain b = authority
# / inertia, and stock gains carry no knowledge of b. On the first X8 export b
# is about 20x below the quad's in yaw, and stock gains put the yaw-rate loop
# below the yaw attitude loop: 50-70 % overshoot on a 20 deg step
# (IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md phase 5, "The X8").
#
# MC_*RATE_K scales an axis's whole rate PID, raised from stock only until the
# rate loop's crossover (b * K * MC_*RATE_P) is 4x the attitude loop's MC_*_P,
# and capped at PX4's documented 5 (the generator reports a cap). Raising it
# further buys nothing and costs robustness: matching the quad's b * K was
# flown, put roll at K 4.8, and sustained a roll limit cycle once a yaw step
# drove a motor to zero, because this vehicle has far less acceleration
# headroom at that gain. b uses the inertia with the arm at its home pose: an
# arm that moves in flight changes it, and these do not follow.
#
# MC_*RATE_MAX is the acceleration available at hover divided by the attitude P
# gain, capped at stock. The allocator holds thrust and backs an axis off once a
# motor reaches zero, and above that limit the P law asks for more deceleration
# than remains: a large yaw step then overshoots by tens of degrees.
param set-default MC_ROLLRATE_K {{MC_ROLLRATE_K}}
param set-default MC_PITCHRATE_K {{MC_PITCHRATE_K}}
param set-default MC_YAWRATE_K {{MC_YAWRATE_K}}
param set-default MC_ROLLRATE_MAX {{MC_ROLLRATE_MAX}}
param set-default MC_PITCHRATE_MAX {{MC_PITCHRATE_MAX}}
param set-default MC_YAWRATE_MAX {{MC_YAWRATE_MAX}}
